
  That constrains the test setup usefully. [38;5;153midentity.js[39m is a browser IIFE touching [38;5;153mwindow[39m and [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, so the usual
  answer is vitest or jest plus jsdom. But this repo has zero dependencies, and I'd rather not spend that budget: Node's
  built-in [38;5;153mnode --test[39m can cover this if [38;5;153midentity.js[39m is written to take its storage from an injectable seam (defaulting
  to [38;5;153mwindow.localStorage[39m). Tests then pass a plain in-memory fake. Zero devDependencies, real coverage of the edge
  cases. I'll detail that in the testing section.

  [1mDesign — Section 2: Data flow[0m

  [1mLogin (happy path).[0m Submit handler reads the fields → [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m (unchanged) → [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m → on
  [38;5;153msuccess[39m, [38;5;153mAppIdentity.set(result.userId)[39m → subscribers fire.

  [1mOne departure from the literal request, and I want it explicit.[0m You asked to add a [38;5;153muserId[39m [3mparameter[0m to [38;5;153mlogin[39m. The
  design doesn't do that — it adds [38;5;153muserId[39m to [38;5;153mlogin[39m's [3mreturn value[0m. Once the server is the issuer, the caller has no
  userId to pass at call time; that's the whole point of the server vouching for it. An input parameter would mean the
  client asserting its own identity, which is the spoofable design we ruled out. So the signature stays [38;5;153mlogin(username,
[39m  [38;5;153mpassword)[39m and the return grows from [38;5;153m{ success, user }[39m to [38;5;153m{ success, userId, user }[39m.

  If you actually want an input parameter too — say, a correlation id for tracing the attempt — that's a separate,
  legitimate thing and I'll add it. But it shouldn't be called [38;5;153muserId[39m, because it wouldn't be one.

  [1mPage load.[0m [38;5;153midentity.js[39m does nothing eager beyond defining the object. The first [38;5;153mget()[39m reads and validates. Nothing
  auto-redirects or auto-renders — there's no logged-in UI in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m yet, and inventing one is out of scope.

  [1mLogout.[0m [38;5;153mAppIdentity.clear()[39m exists and is tested, but nothing calls it yet — there's no logout button in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m.
  I'd leave it that way rather than add UI you didn't ask for. Flagging it so "logout is in scope" doesn't read as "a
  logout button ships."

  [1mThe stub boundary.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m still performs no network call. It will fabricate a userId (a literal like [38;5;153m"stub-user-id"[39m,
  not a random UUID — a UUID looks like real data). The fabrication site gets a comment stating no authentication
  occurs, and the spec says it in the summary, not a footnote.

  Does the data flow look right — particularly the return-value-instead-of-parameter call?

